KickOS: count libstdc++'s references through the __atomic calls on armv6m and RX.

Against GCC 16.2.0, upstream or after the GNURX patches and kickos-rx-irq-atomics.patch, with -p1
in the GCC tree, for the arm-none-eabi and rx-elf families, before kickos-gcc-threads.patch. Neither
armv6m nor RX has a compare-and-swap instruction in user mode, so GCC makes every read-modify-write
of _Atomic_word a call of __atomic_fetch_add_4, and libstdc++'s configure, finding the call in its
probe, counts references under one global mutex. The KickOS user runtime defines those calls on both
as loops over its restartable compare-and-swap. Under the kickos threads model, a multilib whose
compiler defines __ARM_ARCH_6M__ or __RX__ therefore takes the atomic builtins for _Atomic_word, so
its reference counts make the calls; every other multilib is configured as upstream configures it.
The generated configure carries the change its input does. This modifies the vendored compiler, so
it carries GCC's licence, not KickOS's.

--- a/libstdc++-v3/acinclude.m4
+++ b/libstdc++-v3/acinclude.m4
@@ -3813,6 +3813,17 @@
   CXXFLAGS="$old_CXXFLAGS"
   AC_LANG_RESTORE
 
+  # KickOS: the user runtime defines the __atomic calls GCC makes on armv6m and
+  # RX, whose user mode has no compare-and-swap instruction.
+  if test x"$target_thread_file" = xkickos \
+     && test x"$glibcxx_cv_atomic_word" != xyes; then
+    case `$CXX $CXXFLAGS -dM -E -x c++ /dev/null 2>/dev/null` in
+      *"#define __ARM_ARCH_6M__ 1"* | *"#define __RX__ 1"*)
+	glibcxx_cv_atomic_word=yes
+	;;
+    esac
+  fi
+
   # Set atomicity_dir to builtins if the test above passes,
   # or if the builtins were already chosen (e.g. by configure.host).
   if test "$glibcxx_cv_atomic_word" = yes \
